# THE LOCAL PARTS THAT CLOSE A LOOP, WITH THE ROUTE THAT CLOSES IT. One row per (filing, root
# layer) from which following local parts returns to a layer already on the path.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A DOCUMENT WITH NO ARITHMETIC IN IT. Layer A composed from B while B is composed from
#    A means every figure depends on itself: there is no order in which to evaluate the stack
#    and a reader that tries does not stop. Foreign parts cannot do this inside one document,
#    because the cycle would have to run out through another filing and back; local ones can,
#    and that is the price of letting a composition draw on its own stack.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE ROUTE IS THE FINDING, NOT THE FLAG. `CYCLE` stops the walk exactly when a layer
#    repeats within its own path and hands back the path that did it, so a filer is told which
#    loop to break rather than that one exists. An unbounded walk on a two-node cycle does not
#    return at all -- it was measured at four seconds and still climbing -- and a depth bound
#    would only have been a guess about how deep a legitimate composition goes.
-- pm:Part with a local pm:ForeignId, followed transitively; SQL:2016 CYCLE, Postgres 14+.
WITH RECURSIVE
local AS (
    :compose(composition/local_parts.sqlc)
),
walk(filing, root, layer) AS (
        SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_layer FROM local p
    UNION ALL
        SELECT w.filing, w.root, p.part_layer
        FROM walk w
        JOIN local p ON p.composition = w.filing AND p.composed_layer = w.layer
) CYCLE filing, layer SET is_cycle USING route
SELECT DISTINCT filing, root, route
FROM walk
WHERE is_cycle
